Analysis summary:
- In both singular-head graphs, the " is" logit is driven by a set of late final-position features. Some (35_154698, 30_61981, 33_143078, 32_79442, 33_62455, 29_13358, 31_52893, 24_30233) also drive " are" in a plural-head control ("The keys next to the cabinet") — these are generic "predict a copula/verb next" features, so I exclude them.
- Features 28_112184, 30_75672, 35_79277, 33_132106 positively drive " is" in singular-head prompts and are absent at the final position in the plural-head control, making them specific to singular-subject agreement. 28_112184 and 30_75672 recur across the "key" and "mechanic" prompts, and are present in the "banker" prompt (interventions resolved there). Single-feature ablations did not flip the top token, indicating a distributed, redundant mechanism — hence grouping them as one supernode.

